Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 19:36:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] The commit ceiling needs a restart of the box that trades, so
 prepare it rather than take it

WHY A RESTART, tested rather than assumed. The no-restart path for raising CommitLimit is adding
a page file to ANOTHER volume. `mountvol` lists C:\ and two more volumes; one reports the same
1.2 TB / 917.5 GB free as C: (it IS C:'s GUID) and the other is an unformatted system partition.
`PagingFiles` is `?:\pagefile.sys` -- system-managed on the boot volume -- and switching that to
an explicit larger size takes effect at boot. There is nothing to add live, so the ceiling of
245.95 GB (96 GB RAM + 150 GB page file) cannot be raised without a restart. The registry is
deliberately NOT staged: staging it would apply the change at the next UNPLANNED reboot instead
of the timed one.

THE CHANGE, ready to execute:
  HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Session Manager\Memory Management
    PagingFiles = "C:\pagefile.sys 262144 393216"      (256 GB initial / 384 GB maximum)
  -> CommitLimit 245.95 GB -> ~480 GB, against 911 GB free on C:.
  Then restart, then `--verify`.

WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. A restart means terminal64.exe, the gateway resident, every department
resident and every scheduled task must come back unaided, and if ONE does not the desk goes quiet
in a way no dashboard shows. A hand-written list of what should be running is a guess about a
machine nobody re-read, so the inventory is MEASURED on the healthy box (`--capture`) and
compared afterwards (`--verify`): every process kind and every MT5-/E8-/quant task actually
present, plus the commit limit the restart exists to raise. Anything that did not return is
NAMED and the exit code is 1.

It reads only. It never starts, stops, enables or repairs anything -- a verifier that repairs is
a second unreviewed controller of the box that trades, and the gateway belongs to its owner. It
uses schtasks and psutil and never CIM, which has hung on this box. `--verify` with no baseline
REFUSES (rc 2) rather than reporting success: verifying against nothing is not a verification.
A task that comes back present-but-Disabled is reported as not having come back, because that is
the quiet shape of a dark desk.

